JUCE patch: do not crash on a CoreAudio aggregate device that has no clock device and no main sub-device.

Why: AggregateAudioDevice::getClockingDeviceUid() reads the aggregate's clock-device property, then its main (master)
sub-device property, and tests each with CFStringGetLength(). Those properties come back as a null CFStringRef when
the aggregate was created without either one, or has just been destroyed by another process (private aggregate devices
come and go while the device list is scanned). CFStringGetLength(nullptr) dereferences address 0: createDevice() on such
an aggregate (CoreAudioIODeviceType::createDevice -> CoreAudioIODevice -> AggregateDeviceDescription::rebuild ->
getClockingDevice) died with SIGSEGV on the calling thread. With the null check the loop moves on to the next property,
then to the first sub-device, and finally returns an empty uid, which getClockingDevice() already treats as "no clocking
device" (an invalid AudioDevice; rebuild() and toDictionary() cope with it, and String::fromCFString() accepts null).

Touches only juce_CoreAudio_mac.cpp (macOS); behaviour is unchanged for every aggregate that has a clock uid.

diff --git a/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_CoreAudio_mac.cpp b/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_CoreAudio_mac.cpp
index ede3c22da..816fc9de1 100644
--- a/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_CoreAudio_mac.cpp
+++ b/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_CoreAudio_mac.cpp
@@ -1010,7 +1010,7 @@ public:
            #endif
 
         for (auto selector : { kAudioAggregateDevicePropertyClockDevice, subDeviceSelector })
-            if (auto uid = getPropertyOrDefault<CFStringProperty> (selector); CFStringGetLength (uid.get()) > 0)
+            if (auto uid = getPropertyOrDefault<CFStringProperty> (selector); uid.get() != nullptr && CFStringGetLength (uid.get()) > 0)
                 return String::fromCFString (uid.get());
 
         if (auto devices = getSubDevices(); ! devices.empty())
